FUNCTION "GLUE SCALE LOGIC" : VOID
TITLE =Glue Scale: DP Read, ASCII Parse, Valve Control, Alarms FC155
// v0.11 - Simplified "Parse integer part" network: replaced the 7
//   duplicated digit-check blocks (Byte_0..Byte_6, one hand-unrolled
//   per byte, ~140 lines) with a single indirect-addressed loop over
//   Byte_0..Byte_5 plus a short unconditional block for Byte_6 - same
//   LAR1/+AR1/DBB[AR1,P#x.y] indirect-addressing idiom this project
//   already uses in FB1111/FB1112/FC130. Behavior is unchanged: leading
//   spaces are skipped until the first digit (FoundDigit latches once
//   set, matching v0.10), and Byte_6 - the field's last position - is
//   still always parsed unconditionally, space or not. Also dropped a
//   dead SET;=FoundDigit pair at the top that was immediately
//   overwritten by the following CLR;=FoundDigit two lines later.
//
// v0.12 changes vs v0.11 - synced against DB105 v0.3 (see that block's
//   changelog, and DB105_Online_1.xps captured 2026-09-17):
//   - NEW network "Detect scale fault": the KWS CY300 sends a fixed
//     sentinel value (all 7 digits of the Byte_0-6 field filled, no
//     leading spaces - confirmed live at 5222222) instead of a real
//     reading when its measurement goes negative. GrossWeight_Actual is
//     compared against DB105's new NegativeUnderScore_Value constant
//     every scan; a match latches Alarm_ScaleFault. Exact REAL equality
//     is safe here specifically because both sides are integer-valued
//     (no fractional digit) and well under 2^24 - bit-exact in IEEE754
//     single precision, not the usual float-equality trap.
//   - "Skip valve/alarm update" network now also gates on Alarm_ScaleFault,
//     same as ParseError - a scale reporting its fault sentinel is not
//     a real weight, so the valve must not react to it (freezes at
//     last-good state, same as a garbled telegram) until an operator
//     acknowledges/resets Alarm_ScaleFault via HMI (not yet wired - see
//     README "Still open", same gap as the two existing alarms).
//   - Valve control network retargeted from the retired Setpoint_Fill/
//     Hysteresis pair to DB105's new Fill_Start_SP/Fill_Stop_SP: opens
//     below Fill_Start_SP, closes at/above Fill_Stop_SP directly - no
//     Hysteresis subtraction needed since the two setpoints already
//     define the deadband.
//
// v0.13 changes vs v0.12 - HMI alarm reset (per DB105 v0.4):
//   - NEW network "Reset latched alarms", placed after the SKIP: label
//     so it always runs every scan regardless of this scan's
//     ParseError/Alarm_ScaleFault skip state - a latched alarm must
//     stay resettable even while it's actively freezing the rest of
//     this block, otherwise Alarm_ScaleFault could never be cleared
//     once it fires (it would perpetually skip past its own reset).
//     Reads DB105.Reset_Alarms directly (level, not edge) - clears
//     Alarm_Overfill/Alarm_Underfill/Alarm_ScaleFault together while
//     TRUE; each alarm's own network re-latches next scan if its fault
//     condition is still actually present, so this can't mask a live
//     fault by holding the button down.
//
// v0.14 changes vs v0.13 - REAL -> INT for the scale weight/setpoints
//   (per DB105 v0.6 - Michou's call: the scale transaction doesn't need
//   fractional precision):
//   - Byte_8 (the scale's decimal digit) is no longer parsed at all -
//     #DecDigit and the REAL-math network that added it to
//     GrossWeight_Actual are gone. #IntPart (still DINT internally,
//     unchanged parse networks) is now stored directly into the INT
//     GrossWeight_Actual via a plain T - same DINT-to-narrower-type
//     truncation idiom already used elsewhere in this project (FC120's
//     Actual_Velocity, FB1111's StateCode/BadStreakCount).
//   - "Detect scale fault" now compares #IntPart directly (DINT, ==D)
//     against NegativeUnderScore_Value (DINT), BEFORE the truncation
//     into GrossWeight_Actual above - not the other way around. The
//     sentinel 5222222 does not fit in 16-bit INT range, so comparing
//     the already-truncated GrossWeight_Actual would compare a wrapped/
//     corrupted value and could never match the real sentinel again.
//     Order matters here: fault detection reads the wide temp, the INT
//     store is a separate, later step.
//   - Valve control, Overfill and Underfill networks switched from REAL
//     comparisons (<R/>=R/>R) to INT comparisons (<I/>=I/>I) to match
//     DB105's new field types. No logic/threshold change - same
//     open-below-Fill_Start_SP, close-at/above-Fill_Stop_SP behavior.
//
// v0.15 changes vs v0.14 - bridge to "HMI DB" (DB10), per Michou's call
//   to wire this here rather than in FC160 or a new block:
//   - NEW network "Sync HMI setpoints -> scale setpoints", placed first
//     (before the DPRD_DAT reads even complete/validate) - unconditional,
//     runs every scan regardless of ParseError/Alarm_ScaleFault, since
//     it's just mirroring operator-entered HMI input into the setpoints
//     FC155's own valve-control networks read; not tied to this scan's
//     telegram state at all.
//   - NEW network "Mirror parsed weight -> HMI DB", placed right after
//     "Store final weight" so it reflects this scan's just-computed
//     GrossWeight_Actual. Unconditional, same reasoning as
//     GrossWeight_Actual itself (see v0.12: it isn't held back on
//     ParseError either - it just gets whatever the byte parse
//     produced this scan).
//   - Only 3 of "HMI DB"'s and DB105's fields actually correspond by
//     meaning despite both having 5 consecutive INT fields at offsets
//     0/2/4/6/8: Actual_Glue_Weight<->GrossWeight_Actual (0.0),
//     Fill_Start_Weight_SP<->Fill_Start_SP (2.0), Fill_Stop_Weight_SP
//     <->Fill_Stop_SP (4.0). "HMI DB".Actual_Transfered_Weight/
//     Bottomer_Velocity (6.0/8.0) and DB105.AlarmLimit_Overfill/
//     AlarmLimit_Underfill (6.0/8.0) are unrelated fields that only
//     happen to share the same byte offsets - deliberately wired by
//     symbol name below, one field at a time, not as a block copy
//     across the matching address range.
//
// v0.16 changes vs v0.15 - extend the HMI bridge to the overfill/
//   underfill setpoints, per the physical "Glue InFeed Manager" touch
//   panel screen (has "Over Filled Weight"/"Under Filled Weight" fields
//   that had no DB10 backing at all until DB105 HMI DB v0.2):
//   - "Sync HMI setpoints -> scale setpoints" gains two more transfers:
//     "HMI DB".Overfill_Weight_SP -> AlarmLimit_Overfill, "HMI DB".
//     Underfill_Weight_SP -> AlarmLimit_Underfill. Same one-way,
//     unconditional pattern as Fill_Start_SP/Fill_Stop_SP - the touch
//     panel's own numeric-entry widget is the display of record for
//     what was last typed, so no mirror back is needed (matches why
//     Fill_Start_SP/Fill_Stop_SP were never mirrored back either).
//
// v0.17 changes vs v0.16 - drive the real pump output:
//   - Found by cross-referencing "HMI DB" against this block: nothing
//     anywhere in this project read or wrote "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_ON
//     or Valve_Open outside FC155/DB105 themselves - meaning
//     Valve_Open's entire open/close decision never reached any real
//     actuator. Glue_Fill_Pump_ON is the obvious candidate (declared in
//     DB10 with exactly that comment, otherwise unused).
//   - NEW network "Drive physical glue pump output", placed right after
//     the "Valve control" network (still inside the ParseError/
//     Alarm_ScaleFault skip-gated region, same as Valve_Open itself) -
//     mirrors Valve_Open onto "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_ON every scan
//     Valve_Open is actually evaluated. During a fault scan the whole
//     gated region is skipped, so Valve_Open holds its last value and
//     this mirror simply isn't re-run - same "hold last state" behavior
//     as everything else in this block during a fault.
//   - NOT wired: "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_Test. It exists in DB10 but
//     nothing defines how a manual test request should interact with
//     Valve_Open's automatic decision (override it? gate it? run
//     independently?) - left alone rather than guessed.
//
// v0.18 changes vs v0.17 - reconciled against Michou's own online
//   rebuild (uploaded as root-level "FC155"/"DB10", 2026-09-29). That
//   rebuild was built directly from v0.15 - it never saw v0.16/v0.17 -
//   and independently added real logic v0.16/v0.17 didn't have. Neither
//   side is simply overwritten; both are merged:
//   - Kept from the online rebuild: AlarmLimit_Overfill_SP/
//     AlarmLimit_Underfill_SP naming (DB105 v0.8, supersedes v0.16's
//     Overfill_Weight_SP/Underfill_Weight_SP guess), the
//     GrossWeight_Actual_Mem snapshot + Actual_Transfered_Weight
//     calculation (confirmed by Michou: captured "from the actual PUMP
//     Start"), the "RUN_DATA_DB".Web_Velocity_Filtered -> Bottomer_Velocity
//     mirror, and the Glue_Fill_Pump_Test -> indicator lamps network.
//   - Valve_Open -> Pump_ON, Valve_Open_Prev -> Pump_ON_Prev throughout
//     (DB105 v0.8) - per Michou's call: this has never driven a valve,
//     it drives a pump, and the old name was actively confusing.
//   - Fixed: the online rebuild's "Detect scale fault" network read
//     NegativeUnderScore_Value via a raw `L DB105.DBD 10` instead of the
//     symbolic name - restored to symbolic. A raw address like that
//     breaks silently the next time this struct is reordered - the
//     exact class of bug this project has hit repeatedly elsewhere
//     (DB30, DB889) - and there is no correctness reason to prefer it
//     over the symbolic form.
//   - Completed the pump-output wiring the online rebuild left half
//     done: it wired Glue_Fill_Pump_Test straight to
//     "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_ON but never connected Pump_ON (the
//     automatic decision) to any real output at all - same gap v0.17
//     found, just not yet fixed when the online rebuild branched off.
//     NEW network "Drive physical glue pump output", placed after
//     "Reset latched alarms" (i.e. unconditional, not gated by
//     ParseError/Alarm_ScaleFault - confirmed with Michou: "Test
//     overrides", meaning manual test must still work during a fault,
//     not just when the scale is healthy): output := Pump_ON OR
//     Glue_Fill_Pump_Test. Since Glue_Fill_Pump_Test only ever forces
//     the output on (there's no "force off" case), a plain OR already
//     implements "override" correctly - Test=1 forces output=1
//     regardless of Pump_ON; Test=0 lets Pump_ON's own state through
//     unchanged.
//   - The former "HMI Button - UpDate" network's `= Glue_Fill_Pump_ON`
//     coil moves into the new combined network above. Its lamp coils
//     (GRN_LED/RED_LED/WHT_SWL_1/2/3) are dropped entirely, not kept in
//     place - v0.19's new "Alarm lamps" and "White lamps" networks (see
//     below) fully determine all five of those same outputs later in
//     the same scan, so the early copies were always going to be
//     overwritten before the scan ended; keeping both was dead code.
//
// v0.19 changes vs v0.18 - alarm interlock + lamp outputs, per Michou's
//   spec (2026-09-29, see DB105 v0.9):
//   - Overfill/Underfill now interlock Pump_ON: either latched forces
//     the pump off and keeps it off until Reset_Alarms clears it - see
//     new "Overfill/Underfill interlock" network, placed right after
//     the Underfill alarm network (still inside the ParseError/
//     Alarm_ScaleFault gate, evaluated only on a valid-weight scan).
//   - NEW "Lamp scan clock" + "Lamp scan step" networks generate a
//     1->2->3->1 index while Pump_ON is true, using a standard SD-timer
//     flasher idiom (see that network's own comment for the full
//     trace-through) - not bench-verified, confirm the 500ms period and
//     basic on/off behavior on real hardware before trusting it.
//   - NEW "White lamps" network decodes that index onto
//     WHT_SWL_1/2/3, with Glue_Fill_Pump_Test forcing all three on
//     regardless (same override precedent as the pump output above).
//   - NEW "Alarm lamps" network: RED_LED = Alarm_Overfill OR
//     Alarm_Underfill OR Alarm_ScaleFault, GRN_LED = NOT that. Michou's
//     spec only named Overfill/Underfill for RED_LED and "no alarm" for
//     GRN_LED - ScaleFault was folded into both here so GRN's "no
//     alarm" claim stays true; flag if that's wrong.
//
// v0.20 changes vs v0.19 - platform weight sanity check + scale-fault
//   pump interlock, per Michou (2026-10-02, see DB105 v0.10):
//   - NEW network "Platform weight sanity check", placed right after
//     "Store final weight" (reads the just-stored GrossWeight_Actual):
//     the empty platform itself weighs at least PlatformMinWeight_SP
//     (30kg) - a reading below that (Michou's examples: 0, or a
//     corrupted negative value like -20663, both of which a garbled
//     telegram can genuinely produce - the Byte_0-5 parse loop doesn't
//     validate digit range, so a non-digit byte can drive #IntPart
//     negative) means the reading itself is bad, not a real underfill.
//     Latches Alarm_ScaleFault - same bit as the existing
//     5222222-sentinel check, both mean "this weight reading can't be
//     trusted," not two separate alarms.
//   - NEW network "Scale-fault interlock", placed after "Reset latched
//     alarms" (so a same-scan Reset_Alarms clear is seen first, not
//     fought) - forces Pump_ON off immediately whenever Alarm_ScaleFault
//     is latched, unconditionally (outside the ParseError/Alarm_ScaleFault
//     SKIP gate, same as "Reset latched alarms" itself). Previously a
//     scale fault only skipped the pump-control network, which FROZE
//     Pump_ON at whatever it was last commanded to - if the pump
//     happened to be running the instant the fault hit, it would have
//     stayed running indefinitely. Per Michou: "No Pump work!" - this
//     is an active stop, not a frozen hold. Scoped to Alarm_ScaleFault
//     only; ParseError's existing freeze-last-state behavior is
//     unchanged (a different, lower-severity failure mode - a garbled
//     telegram, not a reading the scale itself is actively reporting as
//     wrong/implausible).
//
// v0.21 changes vs v0.20 - confirmed sentinel value + FC156 folded in,
//   per Michou (2026-10-02):
//   - "NegativeUnderScore_Value to -20663 is the INT the Scale gives
//     when Negative Weight is computed! ... no more DINT/5222222!" -
//     confirms -20663 is the real value (DB105 v0.12), not a test
//     artifact as previously assumed. "Detect scale fault" moves from
//     before "Store final weight" to after it, and now compares the
//     truncated INT GrossWeight_Actual (==I) against
//     NegativeUnderScore_Value directly, instead of the wide #IntPart
//     DINT temp (==D) - the DINT/pre-truncation comparison was only
//     ever needed because 5222222 didn't fit in 16-bit INT range;
//     -20663 fits fine, so that complexity is gone. Sits right next to
//     the "Platform weight sanity check" network (same reasoning, same
//     alarm bit, same INT operand now).
//   - FC156 "SCALE POWER-UP CALIBRATION" is deleted - per Michou: "FC155
//     containes your FC156" - folded both of its networks ("Power-on +
//     calibrate sequence", "Power-off sequence") in here verbatim,
//     placed after "White lamps" (matching the position in Michou's own
//     online FC155 rebuild, 2026-10-02). No logic change from FC156
//     v0.3 - same DB105 state (ReCalib_Step/PowerOff_Step etc.) and
//     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Calib_K1/KILOTECK_Power_K2 targets.
//
// v0.22 changes vs v0.21 - per Michou (2026-10-02): "Scale_ReCalib_Req
//   need to power off the scale to recalib" - the power-on+calibrate
//   sequence now powers the scale off FIRST (new step 1, a 3s K2 pulse
//   reusing timer T54, the same action the standalone power-off
//   sequence uses) before engaging K1 and running the existing
//   power-on+calibrate cycle (now steps 2-5, renumbered from 1-4).
//   Previously this sequence assumed the scale was already off when
//   triggered; now it forces that state itself, so Scale_ReCalib_Req
//   alone is a complete "fully recalibrate the scale" action regardless
//   of its current power state.
//   Also confirmed by Michou: Scale_ReCalib_Req compiles to DB105.DBX31.2,
//   Scale_PowerOff_Req to DB105.DBX31.1 on the live PLC - informational
//   only, not adopted into this struct's own field order (all access
//   here is symbolic, and this repo's struct layout doesn't match the
//   live one closely enough byte-for-byte to try forcing those exact
//   offsets without a full DB105 re-export - see that block's own v0.13
//   changelog).
//
// v0.23 changes vs v0.22 - Michou directly refactored DB105's struct
//   online (DB105 v0.14, 2026-10-02: "New DATA_BLOCK 'GLUE SCALE CONTROL
//   DB'!"), removing several fields this block's power sequences
//   depended on (ReCalib_ReqMem, PowerOff_Step, Scale_PowerOff_Req,
//   PowerOff_ReqMem) and replacing the standalone power-off trigger with
//   a simpler single bool, Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse, plus a new standalone
//   Scale_PowerON_Pulse that didn't exist before - FC155 updated to
//   match:
//   - "Power-on + calibrate sequence"'s edge memory moves from the now-
//     deleted ReCalib_ReqMem DB field to raw symbol "FP 201.1" - the
//     same address Michou's own earlier online rebuild already used for
//     this exact purpose (see v0.18 changelog), and consistent with
//     this file's pre-existing "FP 200.6" raw-edge-memory precedent in
//     the pump-control network.
//   - "Power-off sequence" (step-counter based) replaced by "Manual
//     power-off pulse" - a single SD-timer action gated directly on
//     Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse's own level (no separate edge memory needed:
//     SD only truly restarts on a rising edge of its enable rung, per
//     this file's own established idiom - see the Lamp scan clock
//     network). Auto-clears Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse when the pulse
//     completes. Still reuses T54 inside the ReCalib sequence itself
//     (step 1) but now uses its own T56 for this standalone action,
//     since the two can no longer easily share one timer the way the
//     old step-counter design did.
//   - NEW "Manual power-on pulse" network, same idiom, T55, 500ms -
//     DB105's new Scale_PowerON_Pulse field had nothing driving it
//     before this.
VERSION : 0.23


VAR_TEMP
  RetVal1 : INT ;
  RetVal2 : INT ;
  IntPart : DINT ;
  CharVal : INT ;
  FoundDigit : BOOL ;
  LoopIndex : INT ;
END_VAR
BEGIN
NETWORK
TITLE =Sync HMI setpoints -> scale setpoints (unconditional, every scan)
// Only these fields correspond between "HMI DB" and DB105 - see v0.15/
// v0.18 changelogs for why the other same-offset fields must NOT be
// copied this way.
      L     "HMI DB".Fill_Start_Weight_SP;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Fill_Start_SP;

      L     "HMI DB".Fill_Stop_Weight_SP;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Fill_Stop_SP;

      L     "HMI DB".AlarmLimit_Overfill_SP;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".AlarmLimit_Overfill_SP;

      L     "HMI DB".AlarmLimit_Underfill_SP;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".AlarmLimit_Underfill_SP;

NETWORK
TITLE =Compute transfered weight and mirror bottomer velocity to HMI DB (unconditional, every scan)
// Actual_Transfered_Weight = current weight minus the weight snapshot
// taken at the last pump-start (GrossWeight_Actual_Mem) - see the
// "Pump control" network below for where that snapshot is taken.
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual_Mem;
      -I    ;
      T     "HMI DB".Actual_Transfered_Weight;

// Bottomer_Velocity mirrors the 410 seam-AGC subsystem's own filtered
// web velocity (DB640/"RUN_DATA_DB", owned by FC120/FB610) - this
// glue-scale block and the seam monitor share the same physical tube
// travelling through the line, so the same velocity applies to both.
      L     "RUN_DATA_DB".Web_Velocity_Filtered;
      RND   ;
      T     "HMI DB".Bottomer_Velocity;

NETWORK
TITLE =Read 16-byte consistent block (IB272-287) via DPRD_DAT

      CALL "DPRD_DAT" (
           LADDR                    := W#16#110,
           RET_VAL                  := #RetVal1,
           RECORD                   := P#DB4.DBX0.0 BYTE 16);

NETWORK
TITLE =Read 4-byte consistent block (IB289-292) via DPRD_DAT

      CALL "DPRD_DAT" (
           LADDR                    := W#16#121,
           RET_VAL                  := #RetVal2,
           RECORD                   := P#DB4.DBX16.0 BYTE 4);

NETWORK
TITLE =Validate telegram - check RetVal and fixed markers (decimal poin

      L     #RetVal1; 
      L     0; 
      <>I   ; 
      L     #RetVal2; 
      L     0; 
      <>I   ; 
      OW    ; 
      L     "ABC3000A_DB".Byte_7; 
      L     B#16#2E; 
      <>I   ; 
      OW    ; 
      L     "ABC3000A_DB".Byte_9; 
      L     B#16#6B; 
      <>I   ; 
      OW    ; 
      L     "ABC3000A_DB".Byte_10; 
      L     B#16#67; 
      <>I   ; 
      OW    ; 
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ParseError; 

NETWORK
TITLE =Parse integer part, Byte_0-5 - loop, skip leading spaces until f

      CLR   ; 
      =     #FoundDigit; 
      L     0; 
      T     #IntPart; 

      OPN   "ABC3000A_DB"; 
      LAR1  P#0.0; 

      L     6; 
LP1:  T     #LoopIndex; 

      L     DBB [AR1,P#0.0]; 
      L     B#16#20; 
      <>I   ; 
      S     #FoundDigit; 

      A     #FoundDigit; 
      JCN   ND1; 

      L     DBB [AR1,P#0.0]; 
      L     B#16#30; 
      -I    ; 
      T     #CharVal; 
      L     #IntPart; 
      L     10; 
      *D    ; 
      L     #CharVal; 
      ITD   ; 
      +D    ; 
      T     #IntPart; 

ND1:  +AR1  P#1.0; 

      L     #LoopIndex; 
      LOOP  LP1; 

NETWORK
TITLE =Parse integer part, Byte_6 - field's last position, always a dig

      L     "ABC3000A_DB".Byte_6; 
      L     B#16#30; 
      -I    ; 
      T     #CharVal; 
      L     #IntPart; 
      L     10; 
      *D    ; 
      L     #CharVal; 
      ITD   ; 
      +D    ; 
      T     #IntPart; 

NETWORK
TITLE =Store final weight - Byte_8's decimal digit is discarded, INT resolution is 1kg

      L     #IntPart;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;

NETWORK
TITLE =Detect scale fault - GrossWeight_Actual matches the scale's negative-value sentinel (latching)
// Confirmed by Michou: -20663 is the real INT value the KWS CY300
// gives when its reading goes negative. Runs on the already-stored
// INT GrossWeight_Actual (not the wide #IntPart DINT temp) - -20663
// fits fine in 16-bit INT range, unlike the previously-assumed
// 5222222, so there's no truncation-before-compare concern here.
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".NegativeUnderScore_Value;
      ==I   ;
      S     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault;

NETWORK
TITLE =Platform weight sanity check - GrossWeight_Actual must exceed the empty platform's own dead weight
// The platform itself (nothing on it) weighs at least PlatformMinWeight_SP
// (30kg) - a GROSS reading below that is implausible (0, or a corrupted
// negative value) and means the reading can't be trusted, not a real
// underfill. Same alarm bit as the sentinel check above - both mean
// "this weight reading is not real."
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".PlatformMinWeight_SP;
      <I    ;
      S     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault;

NETWORK
TITLE =Mirror parsed weight -> HMI DB (unconditional, for display)

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      T     "HMI DB".Actual_Glue_Weight;

NETWORK
TITLE =Skip valve/alarm update if parse failed or scale fault latched t

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ParseError; 
      O     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault; 
      JC    SKIP; 

NETWORK
TITLE =Pump control - turns on below Fill_Start_SP, off at/above Fill_Stop_SP (was Valve_Open - drives a pump, not a valve)

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      JC    CHKC;

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Fill_Start_SP;
      <I    ;
      JC    OPEN;
      JU    NXT1;

CHKC: L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Fill_Stop_SP;
      >=I   ;
      JC    CLOS;
      JU    NXT1;

OPEN: FP    "FP 200.6"; // edge memory - fires once, the scan Pump_ON is newly commanded on
      JCN   TRXF;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual; // snapshot weight at pump-start for Actual_Transfered_Weight
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual_Mem;

TRXF: SET   ;
      S     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      JU    NXT1;

CLOS: SET   ;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;

NXT1: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Overfill alarm - latching, set on exceed, no auto-reset here

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".AlarmLimit_Overfill_SP;
      >I    ;
      S     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Overfill;

NETWORK
TITLE =Underfill check - start 5s settle timer on pump-off (falling edge)

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON_Prev;
      AN    "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      L     S5T#5S;
      SD    T     50;

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON_Prev;

NETWORK
TITLE =Underfill alarm - latch if weight still below limit when timer e

      A     T     50;
      JCN   SKPU;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".GrossWeight_Actual;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".AlarmLimit_Underfill_SP;
      <I    ;
      S     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Underfill;
SKPU: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Overfill/Underfill interlock - blocks the pump while either is latched
// Overfill: an immediate safety stop if weight somehow exceeds the max
// even though Fill_Stop_SP should already have closed the pump.
// Underfill only ever latches after the pump has already stopped (see
// the settle-timer network above), so "controls the pump" here means
// blocking automatic re-engagement, not an immediate stop - both read
// the same way: force Pump_ON off and keep it off while either alarm
// is latched, until Reset_Alarms clears it.
      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Overfill;
      O     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Underfill;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;

NETWORK
TITLE =Exit point for parse-error skip

SKIP: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Reset latched alarms - HMI-driven, runs regardless of this scan'

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Reset_Alarms;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Overfill;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Underfill;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault;

NETWORK
TITLE =Scale-fault interlock - force the pump off immediately, not just a frozen last-state hold
// Runs after the reset network above so a same-scan Reset_Alarms clear
// is already reflected here. Unconditional (outside the ParseError/
// Alarm_ScaleFault SKIP gate) - per Michou: "No Pump work!" while the
// scale's reading can't be trusted.
      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault;
      R     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;

NETWORK
TITLE =Drive physical glue pump output - automatic Pump_ON, Glue_Fill_Pump_Test overrides it on

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      O     "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_Test;
      =     "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_ON;

NETWORK
TITLE =Lamp scan clock - one-scan pulse every 500ms while Pump_ON (standard SD-timer flasher idiom)
// LampScanClockMem toggles high for exactly one scan every 500ms as
// long as Pump_ON stays true: SD T51 only (re)starts on a rising edge
// of its own enable rung; while LampScanClockMem is FALSE the rung
// stays enabled and the stored timer counts down untouched by repeat
// calls, then on elapse T51.Q goes TRUE, LampScanClockMem latches TRUE
// for that one scan and T51 is immediately reset - which drops the
// rung's enable condition back false next scan, clearing
// LampScanClockMem and re-arming the rising edge for the next 500ms
// cycle. Not bench-verified - confirm the timing on real hardware.
      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      AN    "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanClockMem;
      L     S5T#500MS;
      SD    T     51;

      A     T     51;
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanClockMem;
      R     T     51;

NETWORK
TITLE =Lamp scan step - advances 1->2->3->1 on each clock pulse, resets to 1 when the pump stops

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      JCN   LSRS;

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanClockMem;
      JCN   LSDN;

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      L     3;
      >=I   ;
      JCN   LSIN;
      L     1;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      JU    LSDN;

LSIN: L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      L     1;
      +I    ;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      JU    LSDN;

LSRS: L     1;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      R     T     51;
      CLR   ;
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanClockMem;

LSDN: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =White lamps - Glue_Fill_Pump_Test forces all three on, else scan while Pump_ON, else off

      A     "HMI DB".Glue_Fill_Pump_Test;
      JC    WTON;

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Pump_ON;
      JC    WSCN;

      CLR   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_1;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_2;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_3;
      JU    WLDN;

WTON: SET   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_1;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_2;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_3;
      JU    WLDN;

WSCN: L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      L     1;
      ==I   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_1;

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      L     2;
      ==I   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_2;

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".LampScanStep;
      L     3;
      ==I   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".WHT_SWL_3;

WLDN: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Power-on + calibrate sequence - powers the scale off first, then K1 on for a flat 5s, K2 pulsed 0.5s inside that window
// KWS CY300 power-up defect workaround (Michou, 2026-10-01/02): the
// scale resets its zero/calibration reference every power-up. Closing
// the calibration shunt (K1) BEFORE power-cycling the scale (K2) means
// the scale sees the known shunt reference the moment it boots.
// K2 is a momentary button-press simulation, not a plain on/off - a
// SHORT 0.5s pulse powers the scale ON, a LONG 3s pulse powers it OFF.
// Per Michou: "Scale_ReCalib_Req need to power off the scale to
// recalib" - recalibrating needs a full power cycle, not just toggling
// K1 while the scale is already running, so step 1 below pulses K2 for
// 3s (power OFF, reusing the same T54 timer as the standalone
// power-off pulse below) before K1 engages and the power-on+calibrate
// cycle runs.
//   ReCalib_Step: 0 idle, 1 K2 3s power-off pulse (T54), 2 K1 on/T53
//   (5s hold) started, 3 K2 0.5s power-on pulse (T52), 4 waiting for
//   T53 to elapse, 5 K1 off/done.
// Edge memory uses raw symbol "FP 201.1" (matching Michou's own earlier
// online rebuild, which already used this exact address for this exact
// purpose) rather than a DB-resident bool - DB105 v0.13's refactor
// dropped the dedicated ReCalib_ReqMem field this network used to read,
// and this project already has precedent for a raw FP edge-memory
// symbol (see "FP 200.6" in the pump-control network below).
      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Scale_ReCalib_Req;
      FP    "FP 201.1";
      JCN   RC_STP;

      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     0;
      <>I   ;
      JC    RC_STP;

      L     1;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;

RC_STP: L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     1;
      ==I   ;
      JC    RC_S1;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     2;
      ==I   ;
      JC    RC_S2;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     3;
      ==I   ;
      JC    RC_S3;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     4;
      ==I   ;
      JC    RC_S4;
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     5;
      ==I   ;
      JC    RC_S5;
      JU    RC_DN;

RC_S1: S     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      L     S5T#3S;
      SD    T     54;
      A     T     54;
      JCN   RC_DN;
      R     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      R     T     54;
      L     2;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      JU    RC_DN;

RC_S2: S     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Calib_K1;
      L     S5T#5S;
      SD    T     53;
      L     3;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      JU    RC_DN;

RC_S3: S     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      L     S5T#500MS;
      SD    T     52;
      A     T     52;
      JCN   RC_DN;
      R     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      R     T     52;
      L     4;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      JU    RC_DN;

RC_S4: A     T     53;
      JCN   RC_DN;
      L     5;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      JU    RC_DN;

RC_S5: R     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Calib_K1;
      R     T     53;
      L     0;
      T     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;

RC_DN: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Manual power-off pulse - 3s K2 pulse on Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse, auto-clears when done
// DB105 v0.13 replaced the old PowerOff_Step/Scale_PowerOff_Req pair
// with a single bool, Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse ("Scale Power-Off Process
// (Power Hold 3s)") - no step counter needed for a single timed action.
// Calling SD directly off this bit's own level is safe without separate
// edge memory: SD only truly (re)starts on a rising edge of its enable
// rung (established idiom, see the Lamp scan clock network's own
// comment) - holding the bit doesn't retrigger it mid-pulse. Ignored
// while ReCalib_Step is mid-sequence, so K2 is never driven by both
// actions in the same scan. Clears Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse itself once the
// pulse completes, so it reads as "idle" again afterward rather than
// needing a separate status bit.
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     0;
      <>I   ;
      JC    PWOF_DN;

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse;
      JCN   PWOF_DN;

      S     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      L     S5T#3S;
      SD    T     56;
      A     T     56;
      JCN   PWOF_DN;
      R     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      R     T     56;
      CLR   ;
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Scale_PowerOFF_Pulse;

PWOF_DN: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Manual power-on pulse - 500ms K2 pulse on Scale_PowerON_Pulse, auto-clears when done
// Same idiom as the power-off pulse above, short pulse instead of long
// (Michou: "Scale Power-On Process (Power Hold 500mS)"). Ignored while
// ReCalib_Step is mid-sequence.
      L     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".ReCalib_Step;
      L     0;
      <>I   ;
      JC    PWON_DN;

      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Scale_PowerON_Pulse;
      JCN   PWON_DN;

      S     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      L     S5T#500MS;
      SD    T     55;
      A     T     55;
      JCN   PWON_DN;
      R     "OUTPUTS DB".KILOTECK_Power_K2;
      R     T     55;
      CLR   ;
      =     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Scale_PowerON_Pulse;

PWON_DN: NOP   0;

NETWORK
TITLE =Alarm lamps - RED_LED if any alarm latched, GRN_LED if none
// Extends beyond just Overfill/Underfill to include Alarm_ScaleFault
// too - GRN meaning "no alarm" wouldn't be accurate if a scale fault
// could be active while GRN still lit. Flag if ScaleFault should not
// be included here.
      A     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Overfill;
      O     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_Underfill;
      O     "GLUE SCALE CONTROL DB".Alarm_ScaleFault;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".RED_LED;

      NOT   ;
      =     "OUTPUTS DB".GRN_LED;

END_FUNCTION

